label amir_class
[signal arg="bg:training_hall"]
The training hall smelled like oiled steel and cold morning air, and it was already half full when Lan walked in.
Sixty-some first-years in the Physical Department stood in loose knots, nobles drifting toward nobles, commoners toward commoners.
Instructor Amir walked in from the side door without any announcement, and the room went quiet on its own.
Amir: One rule. In this hall you fight with a sword and with gi. Nothing else.
Amir: No shir, no tricks, no clever devices your family bought you.
Amir: If I see anything else in your hand or in your casting, you get a zero for the day. Not a warning. A zero.
Amir: This is not a punishment. It's a mercy. Shir will save your life someday.
Amir: It will not save it today, and if you lean on it now, you will never learn what your body can do without it.
Amir: Every one of you will meet a night where your gi runs dry and your shir with it.
Amir: On that night, the only thing between you and dying is whether your hands remember how to hold a blade.
Amir: I intend to make sure they do.
Amir: Three styles account for most of what I'll see. Ritterland teaches Fechtkunst, the longsword and the Langes Messer.
Amir: Britannic Arcana teaches the rapier, all point and footwork.
Amir: Al-Tale'a teaches the sabre's draw-slice, a circle that cuts on the way out of the sheath.
Amir: None of them is right. Every one is an answer to how a human body actually moves. I care whether you understand why it works.
Amir: Now, gi. Your capacity is fixed. No training makes the vessel bigger. What training does is teach you not to waste what's already inside it.
Amir: A fighter with a small vessel and perfect control will beat an ocean of gi and no discipline, every time. Feet. Eyes.
Amir: Gi into the legs, and your speed changes. Gi into the eyes, and the world slows enough to read.
Amir: Some of you have had an instructor since you could walk. Some of you have had nothing. I don't care which.
Amir: I have buried highborn children taught by the finest instructors on the continent, and I have watched farm boys outlast them.
For half a second, Lan could have sworn his eyes stopped on him. Then they moved on.
Amir: Pair up. We're sparring.
set {stat_control} += 1
label spar
The pairing wasn't random.
Amir read names off a slate, pairing the middle of the list against itself and the top against the top,
except when he clearly wanted to see something specific happen.
Amir: Schwert, Lan. Rosendael, Leonora.
The room went quiet the way a room does when it smells a story starting.
Lan (Sus): (Rank {rank} against rank three. Nobody expects a contest.)
Leonora (Default): {rank}. Try not to make this take too long. Guard.
- Give her a deliberately bad guard
	Lan (Sus): (Weight too far back. Elbow too high. About four percent wrong. A real amateur would never notice, and a trained eye clocks it in a second.)
- Just phone it in
	Lan (Smug): (Nobody expects a contest. Nobody should get one.)
	Lan (Sus): (About four percent wrong ought to do it.)
She clocked it in a second. One clean testing line, and Lan let his parry arrive a beat late. The flat of her blade tapped his collarbone.
Leonora (Confused): You had time to close that line. You didn't.
Lan (Acting): Guess I froze.
Leonora (Mad): You didn't freeze. Freezing looks different. That looked like you decided not to bother.
Leonora (Mad): If you're not going to try, don't ask to be paired with someone who is.
Lan (Talk): I didn't ask to be paired with anyone. The slate did that.
Leonora (Mad): Then next time, argue with the slate. You're wasting a rank {rank}'s worth of everyone's morning.
She sheathed her practice blade with a small, precise motion and walked off to report the round to Amir, spine straight, without looking back.
Lan (Sus): (That went exactly the way it was supposed to.)
Lan (Sus): (It didn't feel like it had.)
Veldero: Rough.
Lan (Talk): Thanks for that. Really felt the support.
Veldero: I was rooting for you internally. You want to spar? Nothing official. I'm not going to hit you hard at all, honestly. I'm pretty bad at this.
Lan (Sus): (Even a friendly spar means showing him something true about how I move. The safest number of people who know what I can do is close to zero.)
Lan (Talk): Appreciate it, but I think I'll go be embarrassed in private today.
Veldero: Suit yourself. You see Raiz's match?
Lan (Default): Rank one against some unlucky forty-something. It wasn't a contest, it was a demonstration.
Veldero: That's the scary part. I don't think he was even trying. Imagine being that good and that annoyed about being here.
Lan (Smug): Must be exhausting, being a genius.
label courtyard
[signal arg="bg:east_cloister"]
Three weeks in, Lan learned some things didn't need a game script to happen. They just happened, the way ordinary cruelty always had.
A girl was backed against the low wall, a satchel of glass vials spilled in front of her,
one already broken and leaking something that smelled of crushed mint.
Brown hair, fox ears pinned flat, tail wrapped tight, and red eyes bright with fury rather than fear.
Over her, one boot on the satchel strap, stood Han, pale and precise, rank five. Beside him, Jack, rank seventeen, grinning.
Han: Didn't realize the academy let strays enroll now. What is this, hedge medicine? Did you dig it out of a gutter?
Florentine (Mad): It's alchemy. Give me back my[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed]
Jack: Ask nicely.
Jack kicked an unbroken vial just out of her reach, lazily, like flicking a coin he didn't want.
Veldero: Hey. That's not yours. Give it back.
Han: I'm sorry, do I know you? Seventy-four. Seventy-four is going to lecture me. Wonderful.
- Step in between Jack and Veldero
	set {aff_veldero} += 1
	Lan (Default): (Move. Now.)
- Draw their attention with a joke
	set {aff_florentine} += 1
	Lan (Smug): Fascinating. Does the boot come with the strap, or is that extra?
Lan (Talk): He said give it back. I'll say it however many times it takes.
Jack: You're the {rank} who couldn't hold a guard against Rosendael.
Lan (Smug): Funny thing about guards. You only need a good one against someone worth using it on.
Han's eyes narrowed, and for a moment it looked like it was actually going to happen on the flagstones, three weeks in, over a broken vial of mint.
Leonora (Default): That's enough.
The voice cut across the courtyard like a blade laid flat on a table, not loud, but absolute.
Leonora (Mad): Rank five and rank seventeen, harassing a first-year over spilled alchemy supplies. In front of witnesses. In daylight.
Leonora (Mad): Pick up what you knocked over. Pay for what you broke.
Leonora (Mad): If I see either of you doing this again, I will not be nearly this reasonable about it.
Han lifted his boot off the strap, straightened his jacket with two sharp tugs, and left without a backward glance.
Jack followed, throwing one last look at Veldero that promised this wasn't finished.
Veldero was already crouched beside the girl, helping her gather vials, murmuring apologies about the mint.
Leonora (Confused): You didn't close that line three weeks ago. You closed this one just fine.
Lan (Default): Guess I only try when it matters.
Something crossed her face that wasn't quite a smile and wasn't quite annoyance. She turned and walked away, noticeably slower than she needed to.
Lan (Talk): You're Florentine. Rank twenty-six. Alchemy and combat utility.
Florentine (Default): And you're the idiot who almost started a fight over my mint.
Lan (Smug): In my defense, it smelled very expensive.
Florentine (Blush): Hmph.
It got something close to a laugh out of her. Short, surprised, quickly hidden.
set {aff_florentine} += 1
[signal arg="chapter_end:ch03"]
[end_timeline]
